1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,883
Ask around, and product leaders hiring for anything AI-adjacent will describe a

2
00:00:04,883 --> 00:00:09,587
strikingly similar loop: how would you evaluate this, how would you decide whether

3
00:00:09,587 --> 00:00:14,407
to ship it, what would worry you about it, how would you know it's working once it's

4
00:00:14,407 --> 00:00:14,693
live.

5
00:00:14,693 --> 00:00:19,283
Different companies, same four questions, because the underlying job is the same

6
00:00:19,283 --> 00:00:19,914
everywhere.

7
00:00:19,914 --> 00:00:24,562
Most candidates answer all four the same way too: correctly in spirit, vaguely in

8
00:00:24,562 --> 00:00:25,135
substance.

9
00:00:25,135 --> 00:00:29,955
"I'd set up a good evaluation process" is true of almost every candidate in the room

10
00:00:29,955 --> 00:00:31,733
and distinguishes none of them.

11
00:00:31,733 --> 00:00:34,832
It sounds like an answer without functioning like one.

12
00:00:34,832 --> 00:00:39,651
You're not in that position any more, and this lecture's only job is making sure you

13
00:00:39,651 --> 00:00:42,462
notice that before the interview does it for you.

14
00:00:42,462 --> 00:00:46,995
Every one of these four questions already has a specific answer sitting in this

15
00:00:46,995 --> 00:00:51,240
course, built during an exercise, not invented under pressure in the room.

16
00:00:51,240 --> 00:00:56,060
That matters more for this specific interview than it would for most others, because

17
00:00:56,060 --> 00:01:00,650
the interviewer asking these questions is very likely someone who has personally

18
00:01:00,650 --> 00:01:04,494
been burned by a vague answer before, either their own or a hire's.

19
00:01:04,494 --> 00:01:06,789
They're not asking to make conversation.

20
00:01:06,789 --> 00:01:10,461
They're screening for the exact gap this course exists to close.

21
00:01:11,011 --> 00:01:15,741
Notice these four questions aren't a random assortment, they trace the job itself,

22
00:01:15,741 --> 00:01:20,472
in the exact order the job actually happens: assess the feature, choose whether it

23
00:01:20,472 --> 00:01:24,338
ships, name what could go wrong, then keep watching once it's live.

24
00:01:24,338 --> 00:01:28,203
An interviewer asking all four isn't testing four unrelated skills.

25
00:01:28,203 --> 00:01:32,819
They're testing whether you can walk a feature through its actual lifecycle, the

26
00:01:32,819 --> 00:01:37,549
same lifecycle this course has spent ten sections building one artifact at a time.

27
00:01:38,099 --> 00:01:43,141
"A fifty-case golden set, weighted toward the boundary cases, scored against a

28
00:01:43,141 --> 00:01:48,505
rubric two people agree on" is the same idea, drawn from 4.2's golden set and 4.5's

29
00:01:48,505 --> 00:01:53,805
pass rate, except now it's something you've actually built and can describe in the

30
00:01:53,805 --> 00:01:55,033
order you built it.

31
00:01:55,033 --> 00:02:00,462
The second question, deciding whether to ship, catches a confusion worth clearing up

32
00:02:00,462 --> 00:02:05,568
before anyone asks it directly: passing an eval and being ready to ship are two

33
00:02:05,568 --> 00:02:10,868
different questions, and answering as though they're the same one is a fast way to

34
00:02:10,868 --> 00:02:12,484
sound junior in the room.

35
00:02:12,484 --> 00:02:17,590
7.4's scorecard exists precisely because the ship decision weighs the pass rate

36
00:02:17,590 --> 00:02:22,890
against cost, latency, and how bad the failure mode actually is, not the pass rate

37
00:02:22,890 --> 00:02:23,536
by itself.

38
00:02:23,536 --> 00:02:28,707
Naming those variables unprompted is what separates someone who ran an eval once

39
00:02:28,707 --> 00:02:31,357
from someone who's made a real ship call.

40
00:02:31,907 --> 00:02:36,518
The third question, what would worry you, is where most candidates go softest,

41
00:02:36,518 --> 00:02:41,248
because naming a real worry out loud feels like admitting a weakness rather than

42
00:02:41,248 --> 00:02:42,313
demonstrating one.

43
00:02:42,313 --> 00:02:44,145
Isn't it actually the opposite?

44
00:02:44,145 --> 00:02:48,757
Pretending no feature has ever worried you isn't true of anyone who's actually

45
00:02:48,757 --> 00:02:53,428
shipped something, so naming the specific worry is the stronger answer, not the

46
00:02:53,428 --> 00:02:54,138
riskier one.

47
00:02:54,138 --> 00:02:59,104
The fourth question, how you'd know it's working after launch, is the one candidates

48
00:02:59,104 --> 00:03:03,243
skip entirely most often, because most interview prep stops at launch.

49
00:03:03,243 --> 00:03:07,855
Answering it well, adoption, acceptance, deflection, 9.1's three-metric floor,

50
00:03:07,855 --> 00:03:12,584
signals something specific: that you think about a feature as a thing that keeps

51
00:03:12,584 --> 00:03:17,255
needing attention after the demo, not a thing that's finished the day it ships.

52
00:03:17,805 --> 00:03:22,443
Notice the good column isn't longer or more impressive-sounding, it's just

53
00:03:22,443 --> 00:03:23,132
answerable.

54
00:03:23,132 --> 00:03:28,270
Every phrase in it is something you could open right now and point to, because you

55
00:03:28,270 --> 00:03:32,594
built it during this course's exercises or on a real feature at work.

56
00:03:32,594 --> 00:03:37,796
This is 10.5's follow-up-question test again, aimed at a spoken answer instead of a

57
00:03:37,796 --> 00:03:38,986
portfolio document.

58
00:03:38,986 --> 00:03:44,187
An interviewer who's good at this job will ask "OK, what would three of those fifty

59
00:03:44,187 --> 00:03:49,451
cases actually look like." The generic answer has nothing left to say at that point.

60
00:03:49,451 --> 00:03:54,527
The specific answer has forty-seven more cases to describe, because they're real.

61
00:03:54,527 --> 00:03:59,603
This is also why memorising this course's vocabulary without the underlying habit

62
00:03:59,603 --> 00:04:00,731
fails in the room.

63
00:04:00,731 --> 00:04:05,932
"Golden set" and "blast radius" said as terms, without being able to build one live

64
00:04:05,932 --> 00:04:11,008
for a feature you've never seen, sound identical to the generic answer within two

65
00:04:11,008 --> 00:04:12,261
follow-up questions.

66
00:04:12,261 --> 00:04:15,144
The interviewer isn't grading your vocabulary.

67
00:04:15,144 --> 00:04:19,468
They're grading whether the vocabulary is attached to something real.

68
00:04:20,018 --> 00:04:25,137
Notice, too, that the good column answers in the order the work actually happens,

69
00:04:25,137 --> 00:04:30,446
and that sequencing is a signal on its own, separate from the content: rubric before

70
00:04:30,446 --> 00:04:35,186
cases, or measuring before agreement, reads as memorised rather than lived.

71
00:04:35,736 --> 00:04:40,671
Say the honest caveat, because this lecture would be genuinely counterproductive if

72
00:04:40,671 --> 00:04:45,250
it taught you four phrases to memorise instead of four habits to demonstrate.

73
00:04:45,250 --> 00:04:50,125
The actual test rarely sounds like "define a golden set." It sounds like "here's a

74
00:04:50,125 --> 00:04:55,060
feature we're building, walk me through how you'd evaluate it," followed by silence

75
00:04:55,060 --> 00:04:56,012
while you think.

76
00:04:56,012 --> 00:04:58,212
That silence is the entire interview.

77
00:04:58,212 --> 00:05:00,055
Anyone can define a golden set.

78
00:05:00,055 --> 00:05:04,931
Far fewer people can sketch one, unprompted, for a feature they heard about ninety

79
00:05:04,931 --> 00:05:05,644
seconds ago.

80
00:05:05,644 --> 00:05:10,401
This is exactly why the try-this exercise on the next slide asks you to practise

81
00:05:10,401 --> 00:05:12,542
against a feature you didn't design.

82
00:05:12,542 --> 00:05:17,179
Answering well on this course's own examples proves you were paying attention.

83
00:05:17,179 --> 00:05:22,055
Answering well on a feature you've never seen proves you actually internalised the

84
00:05:22,055 --> 00:05:26,871
habit, which is the only version of this skill an interview can't tell apart from

85
00:05:26,871 --> 00:05:27,644
the real job.

86
00:05:27,644 --> 00:05:32,520
This risk is highest right after finishing a course like this one, while the terms

87
00:05:32,520 --> 00:05:36,920
are freshest and the underlying habit hasn't had time to become automatic.

88
00:05:36,920 --> 00:05:41,915
That gap closes with repetition against unfamiliar features, not with rereading this

89
00:05:41,915 --> 00:05:43,223
lecture a second time.

90
00:05:43,773 --> 00:05:48,506
That's the standard worth rehearsing against, and it only works if the feature is

91
00:05:48,506 --> 00:05:49,733
genuinely unfamiliar.

92
00:05:49,733 --> 00:05:54,291
Rehearsing on a feature you already know the answer to tells you nothing about

93
00:05:54,291 --> 00:05:56,511
whether the habit has actually formed.

94
00:05:56,511 --> 00:06:01,303
Notice which of the four questions you reach for fastest and which one stalls you.

95
00:06:01,303 --> 00:06:05,042
That gap is useful information, not something to feel bad about.

96
00:06:05,042 --> 00:06:09,659
It's pointing you back at a specific lecture in this course worth a second look

97
00:06:09,659 --> 00:06:12,171
before the interview that actually matters.

98
00:06:12,171 --> 00:06:16,846
Do this exercise with another person if you can arrange it, since the unscripted

99
00:06:16,846 --> 00:06:20,585
follow-up question is the part that's hardest to practise alone.

100
00:06:20,585 --> 00:06:25,260
A friend asking "what would three of those cases look like" cold is a far closer

101
00:06:25,260 --> 00:06:29,117
rehearsal for the room than answering your own imagined follow-up.

102
00:06:29,117 --> 00:06:33,967
Run it more than once, against more than one borrowed feature, if you have the time

103
00:06:33,967 --> 00:06:35,895
before an interview that matters.

104
00:06:35,895 --> 00:06:38,349
The first attempt tends to expose the gap.

105
00:06:38,349 --> 00:06:43,024
It's usually the third or fourth that starts to feel like an answer instead of a

106
00:06:43,024 --> 00:06:44,134
performance of one.

107
00:06:44,684 --> 00:06:46,505
One thing before you move on.

108
00:06:46,505 --> 00:06:51,530
Pick one question, not all four at once, and actually say the answer out loud to

109
00:06:51,530 --> 00:06:52,472
another person.

110
00:06:52,472 --> 00:06:57,371
Reading this lecture's table silently doesn't build the same reflex as hearing

111
00:06:57,371 --> 00:07:00,951
yourself produce the answer under a little real pressure.

112
00:07:00,951 --> 00:07:05,788
Then be honest about which question stalled you, and go back to that specific

113
00:07:05,788 --> 00:07:06,290
lecture.

114
00:07:06,290 --> 00:07:11,315
The fix for a weak answer on blast radius is 6.6 and 8.1 again, not another pass

115
00:07:11,315 --> 00:07:12,634
through this summary.

116
00:07:12,634 --> 00:07:17,596
Next lecture is your thirty-sixty-ninety day plan: turning everything from this

117
00:07:17,596 --> 00:07:20,987
section into the first three months of the actual job.
